  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Highlight: `dotnet decompile --project` writes a .csproj next to the C#, so the output builds with the right references, extern aliases and target framework.\n\nFeatures:\n- `--project` writes a .csproj for each assembly. Types forwarded into another assembly that clash with direct references now get extern aliases too.\n- `Diagnostics.All` and `Diagnostics.TakeAll()` list each diagnostic with the method it occurs in and how many times. The 1.0.0 counters still work but are obsolete.\n- The library and the tool each have their own README.\n\nFixes:\n- Extern aliases now parse inside interpolated strings, and aliases named like a keyword or `global` compile.\n- Anonymous delegates are written as source writes them, so they no longer fail with CS1908.\n- Lambdas keep their parameter defaults and `params` arrays.\n- Parameter and return attributes, and attributes stored as metadata flags (`MethodImpl`, `Serializable`, `NonSerialized`, `ComImport`, `SpecialName`, `Optional`, `AssemblyVersion`), are written.\n- `const decimal` fields and decimal parameter defaults come back, so they're no longer `static readonly` or missing.\n- `DefaultMember` stays on types without an indexer, and indexers not named `Item` keep their name through `IndexerName`.\n\nPerformance:\n- The native tool uses a fresh PGO profile, recorded for each platform.",
